WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.720
Can one synapse be turned up, and also turned down?

2
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.680
In slices of rat hippocampus, it can.

3
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:19.400
Give the Schaffer collateral input to CA1 cells a fast train, 100 pulses per second.

4
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.040
It can leave a lasting increase.

5
00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:29.520
A long slow train of 900 pulses, at 1 to 3 Hz, can leave a lasting decrease.

6
00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:32.380
Same synapse, same transmitter.

7
00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:34.460
What tells the two apart?

8
00:00:35.333 --> 00:00:37.213
Look at one synapse.

9
00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:43.613
The Schaffer collateral terminal releases glutamate onto a spine of the CA1 cell.

10
00:00:44.573 --> 00:00:46.173
Two kinds of receptor listen.

11
00:00:47.413 --> 00:00:50.393
AMPA receptors carry most of the ordinary response.

12
00:00:51.713 --> 00:00:53.993
NMDA receptors sit beside them.

13
00:00:55.113 --> 00:00:58.833
At resting potential, a magnesium ion sits in their pore.

14
00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:02.413
Glutamate binds, but the pore stays plugged.

15
00:01:03.267 --> 00:01:07.187
Now depolarize the cell while glutamate is bound.

16
00:01:07.887 --> 00:01:10.387
The magnesium is driven out of the pore.

17
00:01:11.387 --> 00:01:16.127
The NMDA receptor then passes sodium, potassium and calcium.

18
00:01:17.087 --> 00:01:21.967
It needs glutamate and depolarization together, so it works as a coincidence

19
00:01:21.967 --> 00:01:28.647
detector. In rat slices, the blocker APV leaves the ordinary response alone.

20
00:01:28.647 --> 00:01:32.967
A high frequency train then fails to produce LTP.

21
00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:38.300
A large rise of calcium in the spine is the trigger for LTP.

22
00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:45.160
In the canonical model, it recruits CaMKII, a kinase that can stay active after

23
00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:50.180
calcium falls. Expression includes more AMPA receptors at the synapse.

24
00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:53.680
The same test pulse now gives a bigger response.

25
00:01:54.583 --> 00:01:56.403
Now the slow train.

26
00:01:57.063 --> 00:02:00.143
It gives a modest, prolonged rise of calcium.

27
00:02:01.023 --> 00:02:06.303
In the canonical model, that preferentially recruits protein phosphatases, rather

28
00:02:06.303 --> 00:02:12.823
than CaMKII. Expression includes removing AMPA receptors by endocytosis.

29
00:02:13.663 --> 00:02:18.983
The same test pulse now gives a smaller response, only at the conditioned input.

30
00:02:19.817 --> 00:02:23.317
Think of a dimmer switch with one input wire.

31
00:02:23.897 --> 00:02:27.137
How much calcium comes through decides the direction.

32
00:02:27.917 --> 00:02:29.997
But that calcium rule is a model.

33
00:02:30.777 --> 00:02:35.837
No single calcium threshold between LTD and LTP is established.

34
00:02:36.257 --> 00:02:38.277
And LTP is a family.

35
00:02:39.357 --> 00:02:44.377
Mossy fibre LTP in guinea pig CA3 does not need the NMDA receptor.

36
00:02:44.377 --> 00:02:48.877
The same synapse turns up or down with its pattern of activity.
